  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)={3}^{3t+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)={3}^{3t+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & ={3}^{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{3t+1}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{3t+1}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\  & ={3}^{3t+2}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & ={3}^{3(0)+2}\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}\u20613\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
